Researchers ran an auction for sold-out basketball tickets. The buyers were business students, people who know exactly what money is worth. Half were told they'd have to pay in cash. The other half were told they could pay by credit card. Same tickets, same seats, same game. The only difference was how the money would leave their hands. The card group bid more than twice as much. Psychologists call this the pain of paying. When you hand over cash, you see it and feel it leave. That small sting makes you stop and think. A card, or a tap with your phone, removes that sting. The money still leaves your account, but your brain barely registers it. Brain scans point the same way. When people saw prices that felt too high, a brain region linked to pain and disgust became more active. Nobody is tricking you. Your brain simply feels payments instead of counting them. And when the feeling disappears, so does the brake. But what if a price you saw first could change what you think something is worth? That's the next study.[0m
